b/ref 1033 261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051050 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d38602f-8b8c-581c-9835-2c6140959f96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059511 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059511 #edb/ref 1071 "Which angles are coterminal with $240^\\circ$?\n\n$600^\\circ, \\quad 300^\\circ, \\quad -480^\\circ$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fae68ee3-73e6-53a1-a141-e362b8118964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059511 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$, we add and subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ\\mathbin{.}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $360^\\circ$ to $240^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n240^{∘} + 360^{∘} = 600^{∘}✓\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $600^\\circ$, so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $360^\\circ$ from $240^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n240^{∘} - 360^{∘} &= -120^{∘} \\\\\n240^{∘} - 2 \\cdot (360^{∘}) &= -480^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-480^\\circ$, so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the coterminal angles are $600^\\circ$ and $-480^\\circ$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059512 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059512 #edb/ref 1071 "Which angle is coterminal with $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}$?\n\n$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e722d3c-d592-591f-a6b6-faac38285630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059512 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi$.\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3π}{5} + 2π = \\frac{13π}{5}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {13 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\nso we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3π}{5} - 2π &= -\\frac{7π}{5}✓ \\\\\n\\frac{3π}{5} - 2 \\cdot (2π) &= -\\frac{17π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac {17 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\nso we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the only coterminal angle is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059513 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059513 #edb/ref 1071 "The angle $\\theta = -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d570998-ebae-5dee-a9d7-8c439225a910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059513 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-435^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= -75^{∘} \\\\\n- 75^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= 285^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ$. Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4f/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059514 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059514 #edb/ref 1071 "The angle $\\theta = \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ede68192-5d89-5204-8c49-a1eeebfbf48c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059514 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {8\\pi}3\n$$\n\nby *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi)$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{8π}{3} - 2π = \\frac{2π}{3}✓\n$$\n\nSo the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2\\pi}3\n$$\n\nis coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {8\\pi}3\n$$\n\nLet's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/23/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nlies in quadrant II. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nalso lies in this quadrant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059683 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059684 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059512 #